these are the faces of the talib  young boys forced onto the streets to  fight for their lives  and some of them are the direct victims  of a climate crisis they have been sent  by their families  from their homes in rural villages all  over west africa  to learn the quran at religious schools  in the towns and cities but when they  get here  many of them are abused exploited and  forced to beg  and steal to earn money for their  masters  we found around a dozen on a massive  rubbish dump outside the city of saint  louis  in northern senegal  it stinks of burning plastic and rubber  flies buzz around rotting food and the  children compete with birds  scavenging for scraps joseph  hasn't seen his parents for two years  and he says he misses them  he spends his days picking and scraping  through the rubbish  looking for small bits of metal to sell  a kilogram  will earn him around 25 cents  that's about 20p once he's found enough  he will walk for five hours in 40 degree  heat  back into the town these are child  slaves and more and more are being  forced into this grim  life the town please  don't know for me  that legendary  [Music]  these photos show talib boys in chains  to stop them running away this boy  managed to escape  with his feet still shackled together it  is a shocking revelation  about what goes on behind the secretive  doors  of the quranic schools  we hear that there are more talib in the  city's fish market and so we go down to  sea  it's quickly clear we're not welcomed by  everyone  it's late afternoon and the boats are  coming in with their catch  talib surround them in the shallow  waters and help the fishermen unload  yes  explained to me is that the talibs are  the ones  yeah rose go ahead you see yeah you see  that  that dean you look there look at that  little kid with two fish okay  let's go with his hands so the talib  come down here  they help the fishermen unload their  fish but any that drop off they take  and then they'll effectively stealing  from the fishermen and they'll sell them  down the market  that's how they try and take money  fish are slipped into pockets down  trousers  and wedged into boots  as the sun goes down the talib head back  to the market to sell their fish  if they don't make enough money they'll  be beaten  it's almost midnight now on the streets  of saint louis  we're with a small organization that  patrols after dark  looking for the boys sleeping rough some  have fled the abuse  others haven't made enough money today  and are scared to return to their  violent masters  but these streets at night are dangerous  for them too  many are raped or physically abused and  the police  just turn a blind eye to it  if the talibs see us white western faces  they will run and so we have to film  discreetly  a camera is strapped to the chest of our  local producer  and they set off in search of the talib  down the small pitch-black alleyways  we have changed their names to protect  them  they are both under 10 years old too  young  to be sleeping rough but they know no  different  they are reluctant at first scared to  leave  but eventually the charity worker  persuades them to come  they sit in the back of the car  wide-eyed silent  and frightened they haven't eaten  properly for days  and their clothes are filthy  we take them to a refuge in the city  where they can spend the night in safety  and have food and a shower  they tell us about the abuse they've  suffered at the hands of their quranic  masters  [Music]  they're here because their families  cannot feed them climate change  has destroyed the countryside they grew  up in and their parents  have no idea what is happening to them  but if ibrahima and ubar go back home  now  they risk being rejected for bringing  shame on their families  and then they'll be back on the streets  again whichever way they turn  there is no escape alastair bunkel sky  news  saint louis in senegal
 

